        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.65,
            0.049,
            0.86,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Proportional Reasoning–2"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.188,
            0.089,
            0.846,
            0.145
          ],
          "content": "each subject shown in the table (each student should choose only one subject). Then write these numbers in the table and construct a pie chart:"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.156,
            0.901,
            0.243
          ],
          "content": "| Subject | Language | Arts Education | Vocational Education | Social Science | Physical Education | Maths | Science |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Number of Students |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_8_table_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.273,
            0.479,
            0.297
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "3.6 Inverse Proportions"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.306,
            0.845,
            0.343
          ],
          "content": "Do you recall the rule of three? When two ratios are proportional, i.e., when"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.346,
            0.845,
            0.411
          ],
          "content": "a : b : : c : d, then $d = { \\frac { b c } { a } }$ . We call such proportions direct proportions. We use this understanding to find the value of the fourth quantity (d), when the value of three quantities (a, b, and c) are given."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.42,
            0.845,
            0.459
          ],
          "content": "？ **Example 1:** If 5 workers can move 4500 bricks in a day, how many workers are needed to move 18000 bricks in a day?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.463,
            0.846,
            0.5
          ],
          "content": "This can be represented as a statement of proportionality — 4500 : 18000 :: 5 : x. We can find the value of x by"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.413,
            0.501,
            0.585,
            0.53
          ],
          "content": "x = \\frac{18000 \\times 5}{4500} = 20",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.532,
            0.584,
            0.552
          ],
          "content": "Thus, the number of workers needed are 20."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.562,
            0.846,
            0.621
          ],
          "content": "？ **Example 2:** Puneeth’s father went from Lucknow to Kanpur in 3 hours by riding his motorcycle at a speed of 30 km/h. If he takes a car instead and drives at 60 km/h, how long will it take him to reach Kanpur?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.627,
            0.801,
            0.686
          ],
          "content": "? Can we represent this problem with the following statement of proportionality — 30 : 60 :: 3 : x ? Will the travel time increase or decrease as the speed of the motorcycle increases?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.815,
            0.622,
            0.902,
            0.687
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_8_image_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.689,
            0.799,
            0.728
          ],
          "content": "The following table shows the time taken to travel from Lucknow to Kanpur using different modes of transport:"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.156,
            0.734,
            0.804,
            0.809
          ],
          "content": "|  | Walk | Bicycle | Motorcycle | Car |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Speed (km/h) | 5 | 15 | 30 | 60 |\n| Time (in hours) | 18 | 6 | 3 | 1.5 |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_8_table_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.823,
            0.499,
            0.897
          ],
          "content": "From this table, we notice that when the speed **increases**, the time taken to travel the same distance **decreases**."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.538,
            0.821,
            0.807,
            0.926
          ],
